af(1,'bm01-sectors','1.1','sl','Business sectors','quaternary sector','Economic activity based on knowledge and information / involving research, information processing or development of expertise.','Explain how two different business sectors contribute to the value of the product or service supplied by {name}.',[
('Mira Sensors','Mira buys refined copper, assembles soil sensors and sells farm-data subscriptions. Its research team develops forecasting algorithms.', ['Secondary activity transforms purchased components into sensors.','Assembly creates a usable physical product rather than merely extracting material.','Reliable sensors enable farmers to measure moisture and purchase Mira’s subscriptions.'],['Quaternary activity develops forecasting knowledge.','The algorithms turn readings into decisions about irrigation.','This differentiates Mira’s subscription from a basic sensor sale.']),
('Oro Cocoa','Oro grows cocoa, processes beans into chocolate and runs visitor tasting sessions. A university team designs disease-resistant seedlings.', ['Primary activity grows and harvests cocoa.','It supplies the biological raw material for the chocolate.','A reliable crop allows Oro to maintain its distinctive local origin.'],['Tertiary activity supplies tasting services.','Visitors pay for an experience rather than a further physical transformation.','The sessions generate additional revenue from Oro’s existing chocolate output.']),
('Tide Materials','Tide extracts sea salt, manufactures industrial salt blocks and employs chemists to investigate new battery electrolytes.', ['Primary activity extracts salt.','Extraction supplies a natural resource that can be processed.','Tide’s own supply reduces dependence on purchased salt.'],['Quaternary research investigates electrolytes.','Scientific knowledge may create new applications for Tide’s salt.','This can open a higher-value market beyond the existing blocks.']),
('Pavo Mobility','Pavo manufactures wheelchair frames, operates repair centres and develops software that models users’ movement. It buys aluminium from a mining supplier.', ['Secondary activity manufactures frames.','Aluminium is transformed into a mobility product.','The frames provide the physical platform on which Pavo’s service depends.'],['Tertiary activity repairs wheelchairs.','Repair restores use without manufacturing a new chair.','Continued mobility can strengthen repeat purchases and service income.'])])
af(1,'bm02-startup','1.1','sl','Entrepreneurship','entrepreneur','A person who organizes resources to establish a business / and accepts the uncertainty and risk of pursuing a business opportunity.','Explain two challenges for the entrepreneur starting {name}.',[
('Nela Refill','Nela has USD 9000 savings and wants to open a detergent refill shop. Suppliers require payment before delivery; residents have never used refill containers.', ['Up-front finance may be insufficient.','Stock and rent must be paid before customers generate cash.','Prepayment makes Nela’s USD 9000 more likely to be exhausted during launch.'],['Demand is uncertain.','Residents may find carrying containers inconvenient.','Nela could hold unsold detergent while still paying shop rent.']),
('Koru Translation','Two translators launch a service for small exporters. Their corporate clients demand 60 days to pay, and neither founder has experience selling to businesses.', ['Cash receipts are delayed.','Work must be completed and labour funded before the invoices are settled.','The 60-day terms may leave Koru unable to meet monthly expenses.'],['Customer acquisition skills are limited.','Good translation does not itself create a reliable sales pipeline.','The founders may spend time finding exporters instead of completing paid work.']),
('Luma Clay','A potter plans evening classes using a leased studio. Only one local supplier can repair the kiln, and bookings are concentrated around festivals.', ['Operational dependence creates risk.','A kiln failure cannot quickly be fixed by alternative suppliers.','Cancelled classes would remove Luma’s income while the studio lease remains payable.'],['Demand is seasonal.','Festival bookings do not ensure sufficient attendance in other months.','The founder must fund quieter periods despite the regular studio lease.']),
('Daro Drone','Daro intends to inspect roofs using drones. Its founder can operate drones but must hire someone to interpret thermal images; several established surveyors already serve the area.', ['A skill gap raises start-up costs.','Specialist image interpretation is needed for a useful inspection report.','Hiring before sales develop uses Daro’s limited launch finance.'],['Incumbent competition limits access.','Property owners may trust established surveyors with evidence of reliable inspections.','Daro may need demonstrations or lower introductory prices, delaying cash generation.'])])
ef(1,'bm03-sole-trader','1.2','sl','Sole trader ownership','sole trader','A business owned by one individual / with no separate legal identity from the owner, who bears unlimited liability.','remaining a sole trader',[
('Sora Repairs','Sora repairs bicycles alone. Demand has doubled, but a delivery contract would require USD 30000 of equipment financed personally. Sora values control over repair quality.','Decisions remain under the owner’s control;Sora can preserve the careful repair standards that attract repeat cyclists','Unlimited liability exposes personal assets;the equipment debt remains the owner’s responsibility if the contract fails','Remain small only if the owner can fund equipment without unacceptable personal risk; otherwise incorporation deserves consideration.','The delivery firm has not committed to a minimum contract length.'),
('Aven Cakes','Aven makes celebration cakes from a rented kitchen. A hotel wants weekly deliveries requiring an additional oven. The owner has personal savings but dislikes administrative work.','Administration remains relatively simple;the owner can devote more time to designing hotel cakes','Finance is constrained by one owner’s resources;the oven purchase could consume the savings needed during late hotel payments','Remain a sole trader for a limited hotel trial if the savings buffer is protected; incorporate before a large debt-funded expansion.','Expected hotel order volumes are unverified.'),
('Milo Maps','Milo independently creates walking maps. A retailer proposes a national order requiring printing before payment. The owner is the only designer and owns a home.','The owner retains the profits;successful national sales would directly reward Milo’s design effort','Personal financial exposure increases;unsold printed maps could leave trade debts enforceable against the owner','Remaining a sole trader is defensible for low-volume digital sales, but national print expansion calls for reviewing liability protection.','The retailer has supplied no returns policy or binding purchase guarantee.'),
('Tala Tutors','Tala runs a tutoring service with six freelance tutors. A school contract requires a deposit for laptops and service penalties if lessons are cancelled.','Fast personal decisions support responsiveness;Tala can allocate tutors quickly when the school changes its timetable','The owner bears business obligations personally;service penalties could exceed the laptop deposit and threaten personal funds','Retain the structure only with manageable contract limits and insurance; separate legal identity becomes more valuable as contractual exposure rises.','The likelihood and size of service penalties are unknown.')],exam='paper-1')
ef(1,'bm04-partnership','1.2','sl','Partnership agreement','partnership','A business jointly owned by two or more people / whose owners share responsibility and profits under an agreement.','forming an ordinary partnership',[
('Vela Studio','An architect wants to join a structural engineer. Both have equal savings, but the architect expects to win most clients. Their proposed ordinary partnership would have unlimited liability.','Complementary skills broaden the service;Vela could deliver integrated designs without buying engineering services each time','Shared control may generate disagreement;equal profit shares could cause resentment if the architect brings most clients','Proceed only with a written agreement on profit shares, authority and exits; limited-liability alternatives merit review for high-risk work.','The expected contribution of future clients by each founder is uncertain.'),
('Neri Kitchen','A chef and a nutritionist propose a meal business. The nutritionist would work three days weekly while the chef works six. Both would guarantee supplier debts.','Combined expertise supports differentiated meals;nutrition advice can make the chef’s products attractive to specialist customers','Joint financial exposure is significant;one partner’s purchasing commitments can create obligations the other must help meet','Agree working hours, purchasing authority and profit allocation before launch; postpone the partnership if these cannot be aligned.','There is no reliable estimate of specialist customer demand.'),
('Faro Trails','A guide and a booking agent want to combine their businesses. Their seasons differ, and they have not agreed how much cash to retain for safety equipment.','Shared finance can smooth cash needs;different booking seasons may help Faro fund equipment throughout the year','Conflicting objectives may delay safety investment;one partner may prefer drawings while the other wants retained cash','Form the partnership only after agreeing minimum safety reserves and decision rules; otherwise a contract alliance offers less integration.','The timing of each partner’s seasonal cash receipts has not been documented.'),
('Ivo Analytics','A data scientist seeks a sales partner. A major prospective client requires both partners to commit full time, but the sales partner wants to retain another job.','A second owner brings selling expertise;the scientist can focus on building Ivo’s analytical service','Unequal commitment can undermine cooperation;the scientist may carry delivery risk while sales effort is divided','Proceed if time commitments and profit shares are enforceably agreed; reject the arrangement if the client’s full-time condition cannot be met.','The major client has not yet signed a contract.')],exam='paper-1')
ef(1,'bm05-private-company','1.2','sl','Privately held company','limited liability','A legal limit on owners’ financial responsibility for business debts / normally restricted to the amount invested or agreed to be invested, subject to legal exceptions.','converting into a privately held company',[
('Onda Boards','A family surfboard workshop seeks USD 180000 for safer machinery. Two local investors offer equity but want seats on the board. The family currently operates as a partnership.','Equity broadens available finance;Onda could buy machinery without fixed loan repayments','Control becomes shared with new shareholders;board representation could limit the family’s preferred designs','Convert if safety investment is necessary and shareholder rights preserve workable decisions; a smaller investment may protect family influence.','Investors have not specified their required ownership percentage.'),
('Pira Pets','A pet-food maker has grown beyond its founder’s savings. Retailers require product-liability insurance and traceable batches. A private investor offers finance conditional on formal reporting.','Separate legal identity supports continuity;Pira can enter supply contracts beyond the founder’s personal ownership','Compliance and reporting consume resources;the small team may need accounting support in addition to batch traceability','Conversion is sensible if growth cash flows cover administration and investor terms are acceptable; otherwise stage the retailer launch.','No budget for the additional reporting staff is supplied.'),
('Runa Apps','Three programmers operate a partnership. They want an employee share plan and outside equity to develop an accessibility app, but prefer to keep financial information private.','Shares can bring capital and incentives;Runa can finance development while rewarding key programmers','Ownership dilution reduces founders’ influence;outside investors may press for commercial priorities over accessibility','Convert with an agreement protecting the mission and minority shareholders; defer equity if the dilution would undermine the product purpose.','The app’s development timetable and future revenues are uncertain.'),
('Seli Solar','Two installers seek to incorporate before accepting a large housing contract. The bank still requires personal guarantees on the van loan, and incorporation has annual costs.','The company has a separate legal identity;ordinary future contract liabilities need not automatically become the installers’ personal debts','Personal guarantees weaken protection for the loan;the founders remain exposed on the vans while paying company administration costs','Incorporate for growing contractual exposure but do not assume that guarantees disappear; renegotiate the loan and cost the administration.','The contract’s cancellation terms and insurance exclusions are not given.')],exam='paper-1')
ef(1,'bm06-public-company','1.2','sl','Publicly held company','publicly held company','A company whose shares can be offered to the public / with ownership divided among shareholders and a legal identity separate from them.','offering shares to the public',[
('Arco Storage','Arco needs USD 24 million for battery-storage sites. Its founders hold all shares. They want patient investment, while prospective public investors expect regular performance updates.','A wider investor base may provide major equity finance;Arco could fund several sites without equally large fixed-interest obligations','Public ownership creates reporting costs and market pressure;volatile project earnings may conflict with investors’ short-term expectations','List if the finance need and long project pipeline justify disclosure costs; phase construction if market pressure would compromise site safety.','No issue price, underwriting cost or investor demand estimate is provided.'),
('Bela Foods','A family food group plans regional acquisitions costing USD 35 million. Public listing would reduce family voting control from 80% to 42% if every new share is issued.','Public equity supports acquisition finance;Bela could buy several regional distributors rather than wait for retained profits','The family could lose majority control;other shareholders may block its preferred long-term sourcing commitments','List if acquisition gains justify shared control and governance safeguards are workable; retain private ownership if family control is the overriding objective.','The acquisitions’ valuations and integration costs are not supplied.'),
('Cira Networks','Cira owns rural broadband networks with uneven early profits. It needs expansion finance and hopes publicly traded shares will help existing investors exit.','Tradable shares can attract investors seeking liquidity;Cira may raise equity from people unwilling to hold illiquid private shares','Disclosure and shareholder expectations can constrain strategy;pressure for quick returns may reduce rural network investment','A public offer is defensible with transparent long-term targets and credible capital needs; private patient investors may fit the rural mission better.','There is no evidence of the market’s appetite for rural infrastructure shares.'),
('Demi Healthtech','Demi plans a public share issue to fund medical software. A founder worries that disclosure could reveal sensitive strategy, and development success is uncertain.','Equity shares spread financing risk;Demi can continue development without adding mandatory interest during uncertain trials','Public disclosure reduces confidentiality;competitors may infer Demi’s product priorities from required reporting','Proceed if sufficient capital can be raised without misleading risk claims and proprietary details can be protected; otherwise seek staged private funding.','The probability of product approval and the listing expenses are unknown.')],exam='paper-1')
ef(1,'bm07-cooperative','1.2','sl','Cooperative enterprise','cooperative','An enterprise jointly owned by its members / operated to meet members’ shared needs through participatory control.','organizing as a producer cooperative',[
('Hala Harvest','Forty fruit growers sell separately to one buyer. They propose a shared packing centre, one vote per grower and pooled marketing. Large growers fear that small members will block investments.','Collective purchasing and selling strengthen bargaining;Hala growers may negotiate better packaging prices and buyer terms','Member decision-making may be slow;different farm sizes can produce disagreement about the shared packing investment','Create the cooperative if transparent investment and quality rules secure member support; pilot joint marketing before a costly centre.','There is no estimate of members’ willingness to commit fruit volumes.'),
('Jori Weave','Twenty artisans want a cooperative online store. Members would retain ownership of designs, share fulfilment costs and vote on promotional spending. Their products vary greatly in price.','Shared services reduce individual overhead;Jori artisans can reach online customers without each funding a separate store','Conflicts can arise over allocation of benefits;high-price designers may object if pooled promotion mainly benefits inexpensive items','Proceed with an agreed cost-allocation and promotion formula; maintain individual channels if members cannot accept shared rules.','Forecast online orders by product category are unavailable.'),
('Kela Dairy','Small dairy farms face a processor that changes prices monthly. They propose owning a processing cooperative but would need to retain earnings instead of distributing all income.','Ownership gives growers more influence over processing;Kela can align product choices with members’ supply and quality goals','Finance remains constrained by members’ resources;retained earnings may reduce farm household income in the short run','Proceed if a staged plant and credible demand make retained earnings affordable; a marketing cooperative could be an initial step.','Expected processing costs and retailers’ purchase commitments are unknown.'),
('Liri Fishers','Fishers propose a cooperative cold store to reduce spoilage. They would share costs equally, although some boats land fish only twice a week and others daily.','Shared storage can improve member returns;less spoiled fish allows Liri to sell a larger share of each catch','Equal fees may be perceived as unfair;less frequent users could subsidize daily users and withdraw','Use membership control with fees reflecting use and a minimum maintenance contribution; do not proceed without agreement on access.','Seasonal catch volumes and electricity reliability are not specified.')],exam='paper-1')
ef(1,'bm08-ngo','1.2','sl','Non-profit social enterprise','non-governmental organization','An organization independent of government control / usually pursuing a social or environmental purpose rather than distributing profits to owners.','introducing a paid commercial service alongside its free service',[
('Mavo Learning','Mavo is an NGO offering free literacy classes. It could sell corporate language workshops and use the surplus for classes. Its three tutors are already busy each evening.','Earned income can diversify funding;corporate workshops reduce Mavo’s reliance on uncertain donations','Commercial work may divert scarce tutors;paying clients could crowd out the free literacy classes that justify Mavo’s mission','Pilot workshops at unused times with a ring-fenced social budget; stop expansion if free-class access deteriorates.','Corporate demand and tutors’ spare daytime capacity have not been measured.'),
('Nori Habitat','Nori restores wetlands using donations. It proposes charging businesses for guided biodiversity visits. Volunteers worry that visitor traffic may disturb nesting birds.','Fees create a revenue stream linked to the mission;visits may finance habitat care and build donor awareness','Revenue incentives could damage conservation;higher visitor numbers may disturb the nesting sites Nori exists to protect','Introduce capped visits outside nesting periods if conservation monitoring supports them; donations remain preferable if habitat harm cannot be controlled.','No ecological carrying-capacity study is available.'),
('Omi Access','Omi supplies free repaired wheelchairs. A hospital offers to pay for repair services but requires guaranteed turnaround times. The NGO depends on donated spare parts.','Contract income improves financial predictability;regular hospital receipts could fund more free wheelchair repairs','Service guarantees create operational risk;unpredictable donated parts may make promised hospital turnaround impossible','Accept a limited contract with a funded parts buffer and protected free-service hours; decline strict guarantees beyond Omi’s capacity.','The reliability of donated parts and penalty terms are unknown.'),
('Pela Meals','Pela provides free meals to vulnerable adults. It could open a weekday café staffed by trainees. The café needs an experienced manager and a kitchen refurbishment.','Sales may make the social service more sustainable;the café offers training while generating funds for free meals','Start-up costs could weaken the core service;manager pay and refurbishment use donations before café demand is established','Open only after a small demand trial and secured finance for both activities; maintain meal delivery if commercial risk threatens it.','The café’s expected contribution and launch cash requirement are not given.')],exam='paper-1')
af(1,'bm09-objectives-swot','1.3','sl','Mission and objectives','mission statement','A statement of an organization’s current purpose / describing what it does or whom it serves and guiding its priorities.','Explain two ways the evidence should influence the objectives of {name}, using SWOT analysis.',[
('Qira Shoes','Qira’s mission is to make durable affordable shoes. Repair expertise is strong, but its website fails frequently. A new school contract is possible, while imported shoes are becoming cheaper.', ['Repair expertise is an internal strength.','An objective to increase repair-service revenue can use that capability.','This supports durable footwear while reducing dependence on competing with cheap imports.'],['The possible school contract is an external opportunity.','Qira could set a time-bound objective for a contract trial.','It must also repair the weak website so ordering failures do not obstruct school sales.']),
('Ravi Books','Ravi’s mission is accessible local literature. Its editors know regional languages, but distribution is expensive. Libraries have invited digital proposals and a large platform is discounting e-books.', ['Language expertise is a strength.','An objective to launch a regional digital catalogue uses this capability.','Library proposals provide a route to readers aligned with Ravi’s accessibility mission.'],['Platform discounts are an external threat.','Ravi should set a realistic pricing and differentiation objective rather than match every discount.','Distinct regional titles may protect its limited funds from a price war.']),
('Sumi Gardens','Sumi sells native plants to improve urban biodiversity. Its plants survive well, but its single delivery van is unreliable. A city grant is available and water restrictions are expected.', ['Plant survival is an internal strength.','A measurable customer-retention objective could exploit reliable plant quality.','Repeat orders help Sumi expand native planting without relying entirely on the uncertain grant.'],['Water restrictions are an external threat.','Sumi should prioritize drought-tolerant stock in its sales objectives.','This keeps its biodiversity mission feasible when customers face irrigation limits.']),
('Tori Wear','Tori’s mission is repairable clothing. It has loyal customers but limited production capacity. A department store offers shelf space while fabric prices are rising.', ['Limited capacity is a weakness.','A store sales objective must reflect the number of garments Tori can reliably supply.','An unrealistic national launch could damage loyalty through missed deliveries.'],['Rising fabric prices are a threat.','Tori should set an objective for material efficiency or recoverable offcuts.','This supports repairability and protects affordability when input prices rise.'])])
ef(1,'bm10-csr-circular','1.3','sl','CSR and circular business models','corporate social responsibility','A business’s responsibility for its effects on society and the environment / reflected in decisions that consider stakeholder interests beyond immediate financial returns.','adopting the proposed circular business model',[
('Uma Lights','Uma sells lamps and proposes leasing them with free repairs and take-back. Customers currently buy replacements every three years. Leasing requires Uma to retain ownership and finance spare lamps.','Product life extension may reduce waste and strengthen retention;repairable leased lamps keep customers connected to Uma','Working-capital needs rise;Uma must finance owned lamps before receiving monthly lease payments','Pilot leasing with deposits and a repair-cost ceiling; expand if recurring income covers financing and recovered materials.','Customer willingness to lease and long-term repair costs are unmeasured.'),
('Vani Packaging','Vani could recover customers’ used food tubs and remanufacture them. Collection adds delivery mileage and needs washing equipment. Two restaurant chains offer to separate tubs.','Resource recovery reduces demand for virgin material;the restaurant chains provide a concentrated stream of used tubs','Environmental gains may be offset by collection costs and emissions;scattered collection and washing could consume substantial energy','Start with the two chains and measure recovery yield and lifecycle impact before broader collection.','No data on washing energy, contamination or total collection distances are supplied.'),
('Wera Tools','Wera proposes a tool-sharing membership instead of selling each household a drill. Bookings peak on weekends, and tools must be checked for safety after return.','A sharing model can increase use per tool;households gain occasional access without each buying a rarely used drill','Availability and inspection costs may reduce satisfaction;weekend peaks can leave members paying for tools they cannot book','Pilot with reservations and sufficient safety-inspection capacity; keep sales available for frequent users.','Peak demand and loss or damage rates have not been established.'),
('Xali Furniture','Xali wants to sell desks made from recovered timber, with replaceable panels and buy-back credits. Recovered wood varies in dimensions and customers expect consistent finish.','Circular supplies can differentiate the brand;recovered wood and replaceable panels support Xali’s sustainability claims','Material variation complicates production;sorting and finishing may increase costs and delay promised delivery','Use standardized replaceable components with verified recovered supply; restrict product ranges if quality cannot be maintained.','Supply consistency and willingness to pay for recovered timber are unknown.')],exam='paper-1')
af(1,'bm11-stakeholder-conflict','1.4','sl','Stakeholder conflict','stakeholder','An individual or group with an interest in an organization / that can affect or be affected by the organization’s decisions.','Explain two potential conflicts between stakeholder groups arising from the proposal at {name}.',[
('Yara Mill','Yara proposes night shifts to fulfil an export order. Shareholders expect higher dividends; nearby households complain about existing truck noise, and workers seek overtime pay.', ['Shareholders may favor longer operating hours.','Neighbours may oppose extra night-time truck noise.','Fulfilling Yara’s export order can raise financial returns while worsening residents’ sleep.'],['Workers may demand premium pay for night shifts.','Managers may seek low unit costs to retain the export order.','Higher overtime payments reduce the margin available to shareholders unless prices or productivity rise.']),
('Zeni Rail','Zeni wants to close a lightly used ticket office and replace it with an app. Managers expect savings; employees fear job losses and older passengers value personal help.', ['Employees seek secure jobs.','Managers seek lower operating costs.','Closing Zeni’s ticket office achieves savings partly by removing employees’ roles.'],['Older passengers value accessible assistance.','Shareholders may prefer the lower costs of the app.','Digital-only ticketing may exclude passengers while improving shareholder returns.']),
('Ari Farms','Ari plans to replace a local seed supplier with a cheaper importer. Customers want lower food prices, local suppliers want stable orders, and growers worry about delivery delays.', ['Customers may favor lower prices.','The local supplier wants continued orders.','Ari’s cheaper imported seeds could reduce customer costs while threatening the local supplier’s income.'],['Managers may favor the cheaper input cost.','Growers require reliable seed delivery before planting.','Import delays could make Ari miss the growing season despite the apparent cost saving.']),
('Bori Data','Bori proposes using customer purchase histories for targeted adverts. Its marketing team expects more sales, while some customers demand control over personal information.', ['Marketing managers seek higher conversion rates.','Customers may prefer privacy and control over profiling.','Bori’s more targeted adverts depend on using the same histories that concerned customers want restricted.'],['Shareholders may favor rapid monetization of data.','Compliance staff need careful consent procedures.','Time spent obtaining permission can delay revenue but reduce Bori’s exposure to complaints and lost trust.'])])
af(1,'bm12-scale','1.5','sl','Economies and diseconomies of scale','economy of scale','A reduction in long-run average cost / associated with increasing the scale of production or operation.','Explain one economy and one diseconomy of scale that could arise at {name}.',[
('Cali Kitchens','Cali merges three meal factories. Packaging suppliers offer a 12% discount on combined orders, but staff now wait three days for head-office purchasing approval.', ['Purchasing economies may lower average cost.','Combined packaging orders qualify for a 12% discount.','If the saving exceeds extra coordination costs, packaging cost per meal falls.'],['Managerial diseconomies may increase average cost.','Three-day approval delays can interrupt meal production.','Idle labour or emergency purchases can offset Cali’s purchasing discount.']),
('Duna Clinics','Duna doubles its clinics and can spread the cost of one booking platform across more appointments. Patients report inconsistent instructions from different branch managers.', ['Technical or overhead economies spread a fixed platform cost.','More appointments use the same central booking system.','The platform cost per appointment can fall as Duna expands.'],['Communication diseconomies may arise across branches.','Different managers issue inconsistent instructions.','Patient confusion can cause missed appointments and costly rework.']),
('Eli Cycles','Eli enlarges its assembly plant and can afford a specialist welding robot. Supervisors now oversee twice as many workers and faults are reported late.', ['Technical economies arise from specialized capital.','The robot can weld consistently at a larger output.','Spreading its fixed cost across many bicycles may lower Eli’s average cost.'],['Control diseconomies arise from wider supervision.','Late reporting allows welding faults to continue.','More defective cycles raise Eli’s repair and material costs.']),
('Fina Foods','Fina joins a regional food-processing cluster where a college trains technicians for all nearby firms. Rapid expansion has also created a longer chain of command inside Fina.', ['External economies arise from the industry cluster.','The college supplies trained technicians to nearby processors.','Fina can recruit suitable staff with less training cost even though it does not fund the whole college.'],['Internal managerial diseconomies may arise.','A longer chain of command slows responses to production issues.','Delayed decisions can increase Fina’s downtime and average cost.'])])
ef(1,'bm13-stay-small','1.5','sl','Reasons to stay small','internal growth','An increase in a business’s size through its own operations / such as expanding output, opening its own outlets or developing its own products.','expanding beyond its current small scale',[
('Gali Instruments','Gali makes 120 custom violins yearly. Its maker trains every apprentice personally. A distributor offers orders for 500 standardized violins, but existing musicians value individual adjustments.','Growth may diversify customers and spread overhead;the distributor adds volume beyond Gali’s current musicians','Scale could undermine differentiation;less personal adjustment may damage the reputation on which Gali’s premium prices depend','Stay small unless a separate standardized line preserves custom quality and skilled training; volume alone is insufficient.','The distributor’s margin and current customers’ likely response are unknown.'),
('Hiri Lodge','Hiri has eight rooms and a strong reputation for personal hosting. An adjacent building could add 30 rooms with bank finance. Local visitors increasingly choose budget accommodation.','Additional rooms may spread marketing and management costs;Hiri could serve larger groups currently turned away','Growth raises financial and service risk;loan repayments remain due even if budget competition prevents sufficient occupancy','Expand only with credible occupancy forecasts and a service model retaining personal care; a smaller extension may fit better.','There is no forecast of occupancy at the proposed room price.'),
('Ila Ceramics','Ila supplies small batches to galleries. A supermarket offers national distribution but demands a low price and identical designs. Ila’s founder values creative control over patterns.','Larger distribution can improve revenue stability;supermarket orders could reduce dependence on a few gallery buyers','Standardization may reduce creative freedom and margin;low supermarket prices conflict with Ila’s distinctive artistic positioning','Retain the gallery niche if craftsmanship and margin are central; accept a ring-fenced range only if incremental returns justify it.','The supermarket’s cancellation and unsold-stock terms are unspecified.'),
('Jena Advice','Jena gives independent advice to 40 local businesses. A software platform could serve 1000 clients remotely but requires generic recommendations. Clients currently pay for personal diagnosis.','Digital growth may generate scalable income;software can serve more clients without equivalent adviser hours','A generic service may weaken perceived value;Jena’s current clients buy tailored diagnoses that software may not reproduce','Preserve small specialist advice unless a tested digital service reaches a distinct segment without weakening trust.','No evidence shows remote clients’ willingness to pay or support needs.')],exam='paper-1')
ef(1,'bm14-joint-venture-ansoff','1.5','sl','External growth and Ansoff','joint venture','A separate business project jointly owned by two or more organizations / that combine resources and share its risks and returns.','entering the proposed joint venture',[
('Kivo Water','Kivo sells filters domestically. A foreign distributor proposes jointly owning a plant to sell the same filters in its country. The partner knows local retailers but wants to use cheaper components. Use the Ansoff matrix in your recommendation.','The partner supplies local market knowledge;market development can reach customers Kivo cannot easily access alone','Shared control creates quality conflict;cheaper components may weaken Kivo’s filter reputation in the new market','Proceed with enforceable quality standards and a pilot; reject if control over components cannot protect the brand.','The size of the foreign filter market and partner reliability are unverified.'),
('Livo Games','Livo makes educational games for schools. A museum proposes a jointly owned immersive attraction using new technology for a new visitor market. Both partners must invest cash. Use the Ansoff matrix in your recommendation.','Combined expertise supports diversification;Livo brings learning design while the museum brings exhibits and visitors','Both product and market are unfamiliar;the attraction may need costly experimentation before visitor demand is proven','Pilot a temporary exhibit before committing to the full joint venture; diversify only if usage and learning outcomes support it.','Visitor willingness to pay and technology maintenance costs are unknown.'),
('Mera Tea','Mera sells tea to domestic cafés. A grocery chain proposes a venture developing tea concentrates for the same national café market. Mera provides recipes and the chain provides a bottling facility. Use the Ansoff matrix.','Resources complement product development;Mera can offer a new concentrate using the partner’s bottling capability','Knowledge may become exposed to a powerful partner;recipe access could weaken Mera’s bargaining position','Proceed with intellectual-property and exit arrangements after testing café acceptance; otherwise use a supply contract.','Cafés’ demand for concentrate compared with loose tea is unknown.'),
('Nivo Laundry','Nivo operates city laundries. A property developer offers a jointly owned service using Nivo’s existing process in new apartment districts. The developer controls access and wants an exclusive ten-year contract. Use the Ansoff matrix.','Partner access accelerates market development;new apartment customers can use Nivo’s established service','Dependence on one partner reduces flexibility;ten-year exclusivity could prevent better opportunities elsewhere','Accept only if volume guarantees and termination rights justify exclusivity; retain flexibility if demand is speculative.','Resident occupancy and competing laundry provision are not established.')],exam='paper-1')
ef(1,'bm15-franchise','1.5','sl','Franchising','franchising','An agreement allowing a franchisee to operate using a franchisor’s brand and business format / in return for fees and compliance with agreed operating requirements.','expanding through franchising',[
('Oli Noodles','Oli has four restaurants with distinctive recipes. Franchise applicants would fund new outlets and pay 6% of sales. Its training team has only two staff and food quality varies across applicants.','Franchisees fund expansion;Oli can reach new cities without paying for every restaurant itself','Brand control becomes harder;weak training capacity could allow applicants to damage Oli’s food reputation','Grow in stages with funded audits and training before accepting many applicants; company-owned growth may better preserve standards initially.','Applicant competence and the cost of supporting each franchise are untested.'),
('Pori Tutors','Pori’s learning centres rely on a carefully sequenced programme. Franchisees offer local marketing expertise but seek permission to replace some lessons with exam drills.','Local owners may understand demand;franchisees can recruit families and adapt promotion in their own districts','Changes may dilute the educational format;substituting exam drills could weaken Pori’s long-term learning outcomes','Franchise with non-negotiable curriculum standards and measured outcomes; decline applicants unwilling to follow the programme.','There is no evidence about outcomes under the proposed lesson substitutions.'),
('Qori Clean','Qori uses refillable cleaning containers. Franchising would widen coverage, but some locations lack refill suppliers and applicants propose disposable packaging.','Franchising can extend market reach with less central capital;local owners finance vehicles and find customers','Supply constraints threaten the sustainability proposition;disposable containers conflict with Qori’s distinctive promise','Expand only in areas with a viable refill supply chain or establish that supply first; avoid expansion based on misleading sustainability claims.','The cost of supplying refill containers to distant franchisees is unknown.'),
('Rili Fitness','Rili operates low-cost gyms. Franchisees would pay royalties and invest in equipment, but several want to charge premium membership rates in affluent districts.','Local investment supports rapid geographic growth;Rili can add gyms without buying all the equipment','Inconsistent pricing may confuse the brand;premium membership could conflict with customers’ low-cost expectations','Allow franchising with a clear market-positioning policy and transparent local pricing; use a separate brand if premium districts require a different offer.','Price sensitivity in the proposed districts has not been researched.')],exam='paper-1')
ef(1,'bm16-mnc','1.6','sl','MNCs and host countries','multinational company','A business operating in more than one country / with ownership or control of productive operations abroad rather than simply exporting.','supporting {name}’s proposed foreign production investment',[
('Sena Devices','Sena plans a plant in a host country with high youth unemployment. It promises 600 jobs but requests a tax holiday and intends to import most components. Local firms fear competition for technicians.','Employment and training may benefit the host economy;600 jobs could develop skills among unemployed young people','Host benefits may be smaller than headline investment;tax relief and imported inputs limit tax receipts and local supplier demand','Proceed from the host perspective if training, local procurement and tax terms secure lasting benefits; negotiate limits on concessions.','The quality of jobs and scale of profit repatriation are unknown.'),
('Tera Textiles','Tera proposes a clothing plant near a host-country river. It offers wages above local farm income but needs substantial water. The government has limited inspection staff.','Factory income may improve living standards;workers gain an alternative to lower-paid farm employment','Environmental damage can impose external costs;weak inspection may allow river pollution that harms farming communities','Support investment only with verifiable water treatment and funded monitoring; reject if environmental protection cannot be enforced.','Water-treatment performance and river capacity have not been measured.'),
('Ura Foods','Ura wants a processing facility in a crop-growing country. It offers long-term farmer contracts, but the contracts require one crop and prohibit sales to other buyers.','Stable contracts may encourage farm investment;farmers can plan around a predictable buyer for their crop','Buyer dependence weakens farmers’ bargaining position;exclusive single-crop production exposes them if Ura withdraws','Accept investment with fair pricing, exit provisions and some crop diversification; avoid exclusivity without protection.','The contract price formula and compensation for crop failure are unspecified.'),
('Vira Energy','Vira proposes a solar-equipment factory using a host-country industrial park. It will train engineers but seeks a government guarantee on its borrowing. Domestic manufacturers already operate below capacity.','Technology transfer can improve local capability;trained engineers may strengthen the host’s renewable-energy skills','The guarantee transfers risk to taxpayers;the government could pay if Vira fails while domestic firms lose orders','Proceed only if additional skills and demand justify the public exposure; limit guarantees and assess domestic capacity first.','The guarantee’s maximum liability and forecast demand are not disclosed.')],exam='paper-1',beneficiary='the host country',decision_maker='the host government')

# Decision trees are shared toolkit; monetary terminal outcomes below exclude the one-off cost.
for i,(name,context,cost,p,high,low,safe) in enumerate([
('Wali Delivery','Wali considers launching a cargo-bike route rather than retaining its existing van route.',12000,0.6,44000,8000,16000),
('Xera Prints','Xera considers launching a short-run packaging service rather than retaining its current print schedule.',18000,0.7,54000,4000,19000),
('Yumi Tours','Yumi considers launching a winter tour rather than retaining its current summer-only programme.',9000,0.4,45000,5000,15000),
('Zora Repairs','Zora considers launching a mobile repair unit rather than retaining its workshop-only service.',15000,0.65,50000,6000,18000)]):
    net=p*high+(1-p)*low-cost
    svg=svgwrap('<rect x="35" y="137" width="24" height="24" fill="#e0f2fe" stroke="#172554"/><path d="M59 149 L250 85 M59 149 L250 250 M278 85 L490 40 M278 85 L490 135" fill="none" stroke="#172554"/><circle cx="264" cy="85" r="14" fill="#fef3c7" stroke="#172554"/>'+svg_text(85,80,f'Launch: cost USD {cost:,}')+svg_text(75,280,'Retain existing route/service')+svg_text(340,45,f'p={p:g}; USD {high:,}')+svg_text(340,145,f'p={1-p:.2f}; USD {low:,}')+svg_text(315,255,f'Certain: USD {safe:,}'),760,320)
    items=[calc('a','Calculate the expected net monetary value of launching.',f'{p} × {high} + {1-p:.2f} × {low} − {cost}.',f'USD {net:,.2f}.'),explain('b','Explain one limitation of using this decision tree to choose the strategy.',['The expected value is an average over uncertain outcomes, not a guaranteed receipt.','Only one launch occurs, so the actual outcome may be the low contribution.','The probabilities are management estimates rather than demonstrated frequencies.','A mistaken probability could reverse the comparison with the certain alternative.']),ten('c',f'Using the decision tree and relevant theory, recommend whether {name} should launch.',name,'launching the new service',f'Its expected net value is USD {net:,.2f}, compared with USD {safe:,.2f} from retaining the existing activity',f'The low net outcome is USD {low-cost:,.2f}; the business may not be able to absorb that outcome',f'A risk-neutral decision favors {"launch" if net>safe else "the existing activity"} on the stated numbers; protect liquidity and test demand before committing.', 'The estimated probabilities and the business’s available cash buffer are not validated.')]
    add(1,'bm17-decision-tree','1.5','sl',f'{name}: decision tree','Growth and decision trees',context+f' The launch costs USD {cost:,} now. The tree gives contribution over the following year before that one-off cost; the existing activity needs no extra investment. No other differences in costs or timing apply. The probability estimates are managerial judgments. Show workings and give monetary answers to two decimal places.',items,svg=svg,check=f'Independent branch rollback: {p}*{high}+(1-{p})*{low}-{cost}={net}; existing={safe}; lower launch outcome={low-cost}. Decision-tree toolkit is shared SL/HL.')

ef(1,'bm18-porter-cost','1.5','ahl','Porter: cost leadership','cost leadership','A competitive strategy seeking the lowest cost position in a broad market / so that the business can compete on price or earn a margin advantage.','pursuing cost leadership using Porter’s generic strategies',[
('Asto Staples','Asto sells standard notebooks nationally. Automation would lower unit costs at high volume but requires a large loan. Rivals already have spare capacity and may cut prices.','Automation may support a cost advantage;high notebook volume can spread Asto’s fixed machinery costs','Rivals can retaliate with price cuts;spare capacity may prevent Asto gaining the volume needed to fund its loan','Proceed only with a credible volume and cost advantage robust to retaliation; a limited segment may be safer if scale cannot be achieved.','Competitors’ actual costs and likely pricing responses are unknown.'),
('Basto Laundry','Basto serves budget hotels. A large central laundry reduces processing cost but increases transport distances and eliminates custom handling of delicate items.','Central processing can reduce average operating cost;large standardized hotel loads use equipment more intensively','Service differentiation may weaken;transport delays and lost custom handling could drive some hotels away','Target cost-sensitive hotels with predictable bulk loads while protecting delivery reliability; avoid promising specialist handling at the same price.','The transport-cost increase and customer willingness to accept standardization are unmeasured.'),
('Casto Grocers','Casto proposes a low-cost supermarket format with few product lines. Its customers currently value local specialty food, while a large discounter plans to open nearby.','A smaller range can lower procurement and stock costs;Casto can order larger quantities of each standard product','The change may sacrifice its distinctive appeal;local-food customers may leave while the discounter retains a stronger scale advantage','Pursue cost leadership only if a defensible cost base exists; retaining a differentiated local niche may outperform direct price rivalry.','There is no comparison of Casto’s buying terms with the discounter’s.'),
('Dasto Hosting','Dasto sells web hosting to small firms. Standardized support and a larger data centre may lower costs, but clients choose Dasto for human troubleshooting.','Standardization can reduce support time per account;the centre spreads infrastructure costs across more customers','Cost cuts can undermine customer value;less human troubleshooting may increase cancellations among Dasto’s existing firms','Create a clearly separate low-cost offer if economies are credible; preserve paid specialist support for clients who value it.','The expected cancellation rate and new-account demand are unknown.')],scope='Current 1.5 growth context plus Porter generic strategies, explicitly HL-only toolkit.')
ef(1,'bm19-porter-focus','1.5','ahl','Porter: differentiation and focus','differentiation focus','A strategy offering distinctive value to a narrow target segment / to secure preference or a premium rather than serving the whole market on lowest cost.','pursuing differentiation focus using Porter’s generic strategies',[
('Esto Bags','Esto makes adaptive bags for wheelchair users. A chain offers mass-market orders if specialist features are removed. The current niche pays a premium for one-handed opening.','Specialist design supports customer loyalty;a narrow underserved group values Esto’s one-handed opening','The niche limits scale;dependence on a small market may make development costs difficult to recover','Retain differentiation focus if the niche covers development costs; use a separate basic line only if it does not displace specialist capacity.','Niche size and willingness to pay for future designs are unknown.'),
('Festo Tours','Festo offers low-impact birdwatching trips with expert guides. A travel platform offers large groups at lower prices. Existing customers value quiet access to fragile habitats.','Expert quiet trips are distinctive;the target birdwatchers may pay more for knowledgeable guides and low disturbance','Premium demand may be fragile;a small specialist customer base exposes Festo to seasonal fluctuations','Preserve focused small groups if the premium funds guides and habitat care; reject platform volume that destroys the distinctive experience.','The elasticity of specialist demand and platform cancellation terms are unknown.'),
('Gesto Foods','Gesto develops allergen-controlled snacks for a small customer segment. Supermarkets request cheaper general snacks made on the same equipment; segregation is essential to current buyers.','Specialist safety builds trust;allergen-conscious customers value credible segregation over the lowest price','Dedicated processes raise unit costs;small volumes must fund cleaning, testing and specialist materials','Maintain focus while trust supports a viable premium; add general snacks only with separately funded equipment and verified segregation.','The cost of separate capacity and future segment growth are unknown.'),
('Hesto Audio','Hesto makes repairable amplifiers for touring musicians. A mass retailer requests sealed low-price units. Current users pay for durable connectors and quick field repair.','Repairability differentiates the offer;professional touring users may value reduced downtime enough to pay a premium','Focused demand limits volume;few large touring customers could create volatile orders','Keep professional differentiation if lifetime service margins support it; do not dilute repairability without a separate market proposition.','Customer replacement cycles and expected service costs are not supplied.')],scope='Current 1.5 growth context plus Porter generic strategies, explicitly HL-only toolkit.')

for name,need,service,cash,challenge,proposal in [
('Isto Access','affordable mobility for isolated adults','a subscription cargo-tricycle lending service',28000,'repairs take three weeks and members miss work','training two local repairers and opening one additional lending hub'),
('Jasto Meals','reliable nutritious meals for low-income families','a surplus-food meal subscription',24000,'donated supply is unpredictable and the cook is close to burnout','signing a paid farm contract and hiring a part-time coordinator'),
('Kasto Learn','access to learning for adults without internet','a travelling offline digital classroom',32000,'the ageing vehicle breaks down and volunteer teaching varies','leasing a reliable vehicle and paying for tutor training'),
('Lasto Water','safe affordable drinking water in remote settlements','a refill-water kiosk network',36000,'filter maintenance is late and collections from kiosks are delayed','training kiosk operators and introducing simple mobile payment records')]:
    stem=f'{name} is a for-profit social enterprise supplying {service}. It reinvests surplus to address {need}.\n\nResource 1 — founder’s note: The enterprise must retain affordable access while becoming financially self-sustaining over the next three years.\n\nResource 2 — finance report: Available unrestricted cash is USD {cash:,}. The proposed expansion would use USD {cash-4000:,} immediately. Current monthly net cash outflow is USD 2000. There is no agreed borrowing facility.\n\nResource 3 — service log: {challenge.capitalize()}. Users value reliable access more than additional locations.\n\nResource 4 — staff discussion: The team supports {proposal}, but needs training time and clear responsibilities. Existing staff cannot safely absorb further duties.\n\nResource 5 — funder message: A restricted grant of USD 12000 is available for staff training only; it cannot pay rent, stock or the expansion. A decision and implementation schedule are required within two months.\n\nUse these resources to develop a feasible three-year plan. Distinguish restricted grant funds from unrestricted cash.'
    criteria=[{'id':'A','title':'Use of resource materials','maxMarks':4,'bands':[{'min':0,'max':0,'descriptor':'No relevant use of the resources.'},{'min':1,'max':1,'descriptor':f'Mainly describes or lists resources about {name} without using them to develop a plan.'},{'min':2,'max':2,'descriptor':'Some resources are applied to parts of the plan, with limited integration of the financial and service evidence.'},{'min':3,'max':3,'descriptor':'Relevant resources support the plan with some synthesis of finance, service and staffing information.'},{'min':4,'max':4,'descriptor':'The resources are integrated effectively to develop a convincing plan; implied use is accepted and explicit resource numbering is not required.'}]},{'id':'B','title':'Business management tools and theories','maxMarks':4,'bands':[{'min':0,'max':0,'descriptor':'No relevant business management knowledge.'},{'min':1,'max':1,'descriptor':'Limited or inaccurate tools/theory; mainly labels or a copied SWOT.'},{'min':2,'max':2,'descriptor':'At least one appropriate toolkit tool or theory/content topic is applied with some accuracy to the plan.'},{'min':3,'max':3,'descriptor':f'Relevant tools/theory are accurately applied to {name} and help develop feasible actions.'},{'min':4,'max':4,'descriptor':'Appropriate tools/theory are selected and applied effectively throughout; analysis informs the actions rather than merely reproducing resource statements.'}]},{'id':'C','title':'Evaluation','maxMarks':6,'bands':[{'min':0,'max':0,'descriptor':'No relevant evaluation.'},{'min':1,'max':2,'descriptor':'The proposed actions are mainly asserted or one-sided, with little consideration of their consequences.'},{'min':3,'max':4,'descriptor':'Some balanced analysis of feasible actions considers affordability, operational reliability or social access, with partially supported choices.'},{'min':5,'max':6,'descriptor':'Balanced, substantiated evaluation compares feasible actions, recognizes cross-functional trade-offs and explains how weaknesses will be managed while preserving social and financial sustainability.'}]},{'id':'D','title':'Sequencing and plan of action','maxMarks':3,'bands':[{'min':0,'max':0,'descriptor':'No relevant plan or sequencing.'},{'min':1,'max':1,'descriptor':'Some sequencing of at least two ideas or functional areas, but the plan is limited or inconsistently maintained.'},{'min':2,'max':2,'descriptor':'A generally logical plan covers at least two functional areas, although priorities, timing or responsibilities have some gaps.'},{'min':3,'max':3,'descriptor':f'A clear, logical, appropriately sequenced plan for {name} links at least two functional areas and identifies feasible priorities, implementation and monitoring; a separate conclusion is not compulsory.'}]}]
    need_theory={'Isto Access':'Maslow: safety, belonging or esteem needs can be linked to dependable mobility and participation.','Jasto Meals':'Maslow: physiological needs include access to adequate nutrition.','Kasto Learn':'Maslow: esteem or self-actualization can be linked to developing skills and realizing learning potential.','Lasto Water':'Maslow: physiological needs include safe drinking water.'}[name]
    items=[explain('a','Using an appropriate business management theory, describe one human need addressed by the social enterprise.',[need_theory,f'Link the need to {need}; the response must describe the need addressed by the service, not merely name the enterprise.']),explain('b','Explain two challenges facing the social enterprise.',['A liquidity challenge exists.','Spending nearly all unrestricted cash leaves too little to meet recurring obligations.',f'Only USD 4000 remains after expansion, enough for two months at the stated USD 2000 monthly outflow; the training grant cannot fund those bills.','A staffing/operational challenge exists.','Training and responsibilities must improve reliability before further expansion increases demands.',f'{challenge.capitalize()}; using existing staff without protected training time could worsen the specific service problem.']),({'label':'c','prompt':'Using all the resources and your knowledge of business management, recommend a possible plan of action to ensure the enterprise’s sustainability over the next three years.','marks':17},{'part':'c','steps':[step(f'A defensible plan first protects a cash reserve and delays the immediate expansion: USD {cash:,} unrestricted cash must fund existing outflows and operating needs; the USD 12000 restricted grant is not interchangeable with that cash. Apply a cash-flow forecast and scenario analysis to decide a safe expansion date.','IND'),step(f'Use the training grant for eligible training connected with {proposal}, sequence training without removing all current service capacity, and assign responsibility for service reliability. The restricted grant cannot fund the non-training elements of this proposal. Apply relevant motivation/training theory or a business plan to link staff capability to the social mission.','IND'),step(f'Pilot one operational improvement before a new location, compare affordable user charges or partnership support with the risk of excluding users, and monitor monthly liquidity, service completion and access for the target users. Different feasible actions are valid.','IND'),step('Evaluate finance–operations–HR trade-offs: delaying expansion protects liquidity and reliability but postpones access for new users; training improves long-term service while temporarily consuming staff time. Set a review point, fallback cash threshold and responsibility for decisions. Critiquing the usefulness of the sources is not required for criterion C.','IND')],'rubric':{'maxMarks':17,'criteria':criteria}})]
    # Service flow is a neutral stimulus, not an annotated solution.
    body=''
    for x,t in [(20,'Affordable subscription'),(265,'Shared service access'),(510,'Maintenance / support')]:
        body+=f'<rect x="{x}" y="30" width="220" height="70" rx="8" fill="#e0f2fe" stroke="#1e3a8a"/>'+svg_text(x+10,69,t,14)
    body+='<path d="M240 65 L265 65 M485 65 L510 65" fill="none" stroke="#1e3a8a"/>'
    add(1,'bm20-social-enterprise-plan','1.2','ahl',f'{name}: three-year social-enterprise plan','HL Paper 3 social enterprise',stem,items,'paper-3',kind='extended-response',svg=svgwrap(body,760,130),check='Cash after immediate expansion=USD4000; at USD2000 net monthly outflow this is two months. Restricted training grant excluded from operating liquidity. Separate A4+B4+C6+D3 criteria sum17; a2+b6+c17=25.',scope='Shared 1.2 social enterprise content; highest demand is explicitly HL-only Paper 3 decision-making plan.')
